\description{
Ordinates samples straight from a count table, treating zeros as missing
values rather than replacing them with pseudocounts. Robust Aitchison PCA
(Martino et al. 2019) is the method of DEICODE and of QIIME 2's \code{gemelli}
plugin: a robust centered log-ratio transform of the non-zero counts,
completion of the missing entries with a low-rank matrix (OptSpace), and a
PCA of the completed matrix. It returns sample scores and feature loadings
for a biplot.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Filtering.} Features are kept if their total count is above
\code{min_feature_count} and they are non-zero in more than
\code{min_feature_frequency} percent of samples; then samples are kept if
their total over those features is above \code{min_sample_count}; then
features that are zero in every remaining sample are dropped. These
are gemelli's filters with its defaults: at \code{0}, they only drop
samples and features that are entirely zero. (gemelli's documentation
suggests a \code{min_sample_count} of at least 500.) Samples with a single
non-zero feature are also dropped, with a warning: see "Differences
from gemelli". The result has rows for the kept samples only.
\item \strong{Robust clr.} For each sample, the log of each non-zero count minus
the mean log over its non-zero counts (as \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}} with
\code{norm = 'rclr'}). Zeros are missing values: they are not part of the
mean and are not given a value.
\item \strong{Matrix completion.} OptSpace (Keshavan, Montanari & Oh 2010) fits a
rank-\code{n_axes} matrix \eqn{USV^T} to the observed values only: it starts from
their top \code{n_axes} singular vectors and runs up to \code{max_iters} steps of
gradient descent on the Grassmann manifold, re-fitting the
\eqn{k \times k} matrix \eqn{S} after each step.
\item \strong{PCA.} The completed matrix \eqn{X = USV^T} is centered (columns,
then rows) and decomposed as \eqn{X = u\,\mathrm{diag}(s)\,v^T}. The
sample scores are \eqn{u\,\mathrm{diag}(s)} and the feature loadings
\eqn{v}. So \code{ord \%*\% t(attr(ord, 'loadings'))} is the centered
completed matrix, and \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} of the Euclidean distances between its
rows would give the same scores.
}

\code{rpca()} takes a count table, not distances, so like \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} it is called
directly rather than through \code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}.

\strong{Relation to gemelli's results}

gemelli reports the singular values \eqn{s} as its "eigenvalues", the
unscaled \eqn{u} as its sample loadings, and \eqn{s^2 / \sum s^2} as the
proportion explained. Here \code{eig} is a variance, \eqn{s^2 / (n - 1)}, on the
same scale as \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}'s, and the scores are \eqn{u\,\mathrm{diag}(s)}, as
for \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}. So, for an ordination \code{ord} of \eqn{n} samples,
gemelli's eigenvalues are \code{sqrt(attr(ord, 'eig') * (n - 1))} and its
sample loadings are \code{sweep(ord, 2, sqrt(attr(ord, 'eig') * (n - 1)), '/')}.
\code{prop} and the feature loadings are the same as gemelli's.

\strong{Speed and memory}

gemelli works with dense \eqn{n \times p} arrays. \code{rpca()} never forms
one: every step works from the non-zero counts and the
\eqn{n \times k} and \eqn{p \times k} factors, in parallel C. The initial
singular vectors come from block Lanczos iteration using sparse products
(the same solver as \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}); the descent's cost and
gradients are sums over the non-zero counts; the line search's cost along
the search direction is a quartic polynomial whose coefficients take one
pass; and the final PCA reduces to \eqn{k \times k} problems.

Each descent step takes time proportional to the number of non-zero
counts times \eqn{k^2}, plus \eqn{k^4/4} per sample for re-fitting
\eqn{S}. The initial SVD takes a number of steps that depends on the gap
between the \code{n_axes}-th and next singular values, each a pass over the
non-zero counts plus work on a basis of \eqn{\min(n, p)}-long vectors.
Memory beyond the input is about 24 bytes per non-zero count, plus about
\eqn{80 (n + p) k} bytes for the factors, plus about 1.5 kB per sample or
per feature, whichever there are fewer of, for the Lanczos basis.

Threads (\code{cpus}) share every pass over the non-zero counts; small tables
use one thread. Results are identical from run to run and for any number
of \code{cpus}; R's random number generator is neither used nor changed. Each
axis is flipped so that its largest score in absolute value is positive,
with the loadings flipped to match.

\strong{Biplots}

The scores are \eqn{u\,\mathrm{diag}(s)} and the loadings are the unit
vectors \eqn{v}: a distance (form) biplot, in which distances between
samples are preserved and each feature's arrow shows the direction in
which its robust clr increases. (gemelli, and QIIME 2's Emperor, plot
\eqn{u} and \eqn{v}.) The loadings are much shorter than the scores;
\code{plot(ord)} scales them to fit among the samples and draws them as
arrows (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ord <- rpca(ex_counts, n_axes = 2)
plot(ord)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Iterations}

OptSpace minimizes the error on the observed values only, so with many
iterations it can overfit them, and the completed values (and the
eigenvalues) can grow far beyond the data. For example, on a 20
\eqn{\times} 10 table with half its counts zero, the first singular value
was 15.5 after 5 iterations, 30.2 after 50, and 41.7 after 400; gemelli's
own OptSpace behaves the same. The default of 5 is gemelli's and is
recommended; \code{cost} shows how the fit changes.

\strong{Distances}

With \code{distances = TRUE}, \code{rpca()} also returns the distances that gemelli
returns with its ordination (which it describes as the Aitchison distance
of the sample loadings): Euclidean distances between the rows of
OptSpace's sample factor \eqn{U}, which are normalized so that each of its
\code{n_axes} columns has a squared length of about \eqn{n}, before the singular
values in \eqn{S} are applied. All \code{n_axes} axes count equally in them. They
are computed in parallel straight into a \code{dist} object, so they can go to
\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}} or \code{\link[=permanova]{permanova()}}, and nothing of size
\eqn{n \times n} is made without \code{distances = TRUE}.

They differ from two other distances:
\itemize{
\item \code{dist(ord)}: Euclidean distances between the returned scores, i.e.
between the samples' rows of the centered completed matrix, where each
axis counts in proportion to its variance.
\item \code{\link[=robust_aitchison]{robust_aitchison()}}: Euclidean distances between the samples' robust
clr values with no completion: missing values count as 0, so each pair
of samples is compared on the features observed in either, and no
low-rank model is fitted. For a table with no zeros and \code{n_axes} at least
the rank of its clr matrix, the completed matrix is the clr matrix, so
\code{dist(ord)} is then the same as \code{robust_aitchison()} and \code{\link[=aitchison]{aitchison()}}.
}

\strong{Differences from gemelli}

\code{rpca()} is a port of \code{gemelli.rpca.rpca()} (gemelli 0.0.13), and gives
its results to within rounding error and the initial SVD's tolerance, with
these exceptions. On the package's test tables, results agree with
gemelli's to about \eqn{10^{-13}} (relative). Ill-conditioned problems
agree less closely: when the \code{n_axes}-th and next singular values of the rclr
matrix are nearly tied, the initial subspace (in gemelli as here) is only
determined to about \eqn{10^{-15}} divided by their relative gap, and
results can then differ from gemelli's by \eqn{10^{-10}} or more.
\itemize{
\item gemelli's OptSpace treats any robust clr value that is exactly 0 as
missing, which depends on rounding. Here a value is missing exactly
when its count is 0.
\item gemelli keeps samples with a single non-zero feature. Their robust clr
is 0, which gemelli treats as missing, so they have no observed values
and their position only reflects the other samples. \code{rpca()} drops them
with a warning.
\item gemelli requires \eqn{2 \le k \le \min(n, p) - 1}. \code{rpca()} accepts
\code{n_axes = 1}, and reduces \code{n_axes} above \eqn{\min(n, p) - 1} with a warning.
It also limits \code{n_axes} to 10, because re-fitting \eqn{S} takes time in
proportion to \eqn{k^4}; gemelli has no limit but suggests
\eqn{k < 10}. gemelli adds an axis of zeros when \eqn{k = 2};
\code{rpca()} doesn't.
\item gemelli's \code{rpca()} takes \code{max_iterations} but doesn't pass it on, so it
always runs 5 iterations; here \code{max_iters} is used. \code{tol} is OptSpace's
own.
\item \code{eig} and the scores are scaled as described in "Relation to gemelli's
results".
\item Axes whose variance is at rounding-error level (below
\code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} times the larger of the first axis's and
the rclr values' sum of squares, i.e. variation below about
\eqn{10^{-4}} of the data's in amplitude) are dropped with a warning;
if no axis is left (e.g. all samples have the same composition),
\code{rpca()} stops with an error. gemelli returns such axes.
\item The line search's trial costs are computed from a polynomial rather
than by re-summing, which differs only by rounding; in a borderline
case that can change which step length is accepted.
}
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them. The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    ord <- rpca(iris[, 1:4])
    plot(ord, 'Species', iris)
    head(ord)
    str(ord)

}
